{"n": 1240, "w": 1000, "h": 764, "img": "1240.webp", "text": "65-115\n\nTRIMMING RADIO\n\n1. Pull out antenna to length of about 0.9 ... 1.0 meters (35 to 39”), making sure that bottom\ntelescope section is pulled out fully.\n\n2. Set medium wave range.(AM) of radio to a weak reception station at 1100 KHZ. Now turn\nthe antenna trimmer until the maximum loudness is reached.\nShould there not be station at 1100 kHZ, it would be sufficient wo trim the background\nnoise to maximum loudness,\nTo check whether trimming is correct, turn the antenna trimmer.around its axis once. Now\nthe maximum loudness should be found at two different positions of the trimmer.\nAt these points there is the greatest input voltage and the greatest resonance. The trimmer\nmust be set to one of both positions.\n\nNOTE:\n\nMake sure that the antonna is \"grounded\" correctiy; remove paint, Teroson or rust and coat\ncleaned surfaces with graphite grease for protection against corrosion,\n\n73965140\n\nINFLUENCES ON RADIO RECEPTION.\nWHILE DRIVING\n\nIn spite of en accurately trimmed antenna, good\n\nFLUTTER\n\nThis noise will usually occur because af shade\noff. The ditect station/radio connection will be\ninterrupted by a natural or artificial obstacte,\nWhen driving down an avenue this is known as\nthe “lattice effect”.\n\nHISS, CRACKLE AND SPLASH\n\nThese noises will usually be caused by reflection.\nReflections occur, when the same station signals\nare received in the car radio two or many times\n\nwithin a fraction of a second.\n\nThe quickly changing loudness is very disturbing.\nAn optical comparision would be the “ghost on\nthe TV screen, which is also produced by reflec-\ntions,", "words": [["65-115", 0.4672, 0.0165, 0.5389, 0.0403], ["TRIMMING", 0.0217, 0.0694, 0.0818, 0.082], ["RADIO", 0.0859, 0.0707, 0.1202, 0.0833], ["1.", 0.0227, 0.1018, 0.0318, 0.1137], ["Pull", 0.0419, 0.0991, 0.0601, 0.1229], ["out", 0.0646, 0.0991, 0.0783, 0.1229], ["antenna", 0.0828, 0.0991, 0.1177, 0.1229],